C.4 Grouped Objective L79:   13. cite69†D Label Construction L80:     1. cite70†D.1 Canonical Predicate Interface L81:     2. cite71†D.2 Phase Construction L82:     3. cite72†D.3 Preservation and Counterfactual Labels L83:   14. cite73†E Evaluation Protocol L84:     1. cite74†E.1 Training and Checkpoint Selection L85:       1. cite75†Data Pools and Dynamic Resampling. L86:       2. cite76†Acceptance Criteria. L87:     2. cite77†E.2 Closed-Loop Inference L88:       1. cite78†Stage-Aware Candidate Scoring. L89:       2. cite79†Violation-Aware Suffix Repair. L90:       3. cite80†Candidate Accounting. L91:     3. cite81†E.3 LIBERO Evaluation L92:       1. cite82†LIBERO Splits and Rollout Matching. L93:       2. cite83†Primary Metrics. L94:       3. cite84†Candidate Diversity. L95:       4. cite85†Representation Comparisons and Causal Scope. L96:       5. cite86†Mask-Dependence Diagnostics. L97:       6. cite87†Statistical Reporting. L98:   15. cite88†F Additional Analysis L99:     1. cite89†F.1 Result Provenance L100:     2. cite90†F.2 Failure Taxonomy and Qualitative Analysis L101:   16. cite91†G Physical-System Protocol L102:     1. cite92†G.1 Observation and State Contract L103:     2. cite93†G.2 Provider Validation L104:     3. cite94†G.3 Deployment Gates L105:   17. cite95†H Reproducibility Package L106:   18. cite96†I Supplementary Limitations L107: cite97†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L108: \nL109: arXiv:2608.22294v1 [cs.RO] 23 Aug 2026\nL110: # Beyond Instance Slots: Semantically Rich World Models for Physical Interaction Planning\nL111: \nL112: Juntao Cheng Thanks: Work done during the internship at Beijing Academy of Artificial Intelligence (BAAI).    Jingkai Wang    Yijun Shen    Xiansheng Chen    Zhiwei Yu\\corresponding\nL113: ###### Abstract\nL114: World models for physical interaction are typically trained to predict future observations or latent features; however, a planning-oriented model must answer a fundamentally different question: whether a candidate action produces a task-consistent future while preserving essential relations. Monolithic state representations obscure the underlying entities, while standard instance-level object slots merely identify what is present without specifying what role each entity plays in the task context.\nL115: To bridge this gap, we present the Semantically Rich World Model (SR-WM), a task-conditioned world model structured around five functional roles: gripper, target, goal, relation, and phase. Within SR-WM, a visual entity encoder extracts soft entity hypotheses from pretrained patch features, allowing segmentation masks to serve as optional proposal priors without mandating them as required state representations or inference inputs.\nL116: A role binder subsequently maps these hypotheses to task-specific roles, while an action-conditioned dynamics model predicts role transitions alongside fine-grained semantics, including grasp/contact, predicate establishment, relation preservation, fixture state, and phase change. Crucially, this unified role state grounds downstream multi-candidate action generation, stage-aware reranking, and violation-aware suffix resampling.\nL117: Our comprehensive evaluation protocol spans all four LIBERO simulation suites, cross-suite transfer, perception diagnostics, and action-sensitivity analysis. Ultimately, this formulation transforms object-centric prediction into a semantic interface linking visual dynamics with planning-oriented decision making.\nL118: ## 1 Introduction\nL119: World models predict how environments evolve under candidate actions, and recent latent models make this prediction efficient (cite98†16 ; cite99†11 ). Yet low prediction error does not ensure a useful decision: a plausible future may approach the wrong object, lose a grasp, undo an opened fixture, or release too early. Planning must therefore ask whether an action establishes the required state while preserving satisfied constraints.\nL120: Existing planning-aligned objectives expose this gap but still leave task semantics largely implicit (cite100†6 ).\nL121: Object-centric states separate scenes into entities and improve robustness, exploration, and planning (cite101†10 ; cite102†2 ; cite103†17 ; cite104†14 ), but entity identity does not determine task role. A carrot and container do not reveal which is the target or goal, whether grasp holds, which predicate is required, or when to release. Because an object may change roles across instructions, exchangeable instance slots leave task binding and progress to the downstream policy.\nL122: We propose the Semantically Rich World Model (SR-WM), a compact architecture built on the 15M-parameter LeWM encoder–predictor (cite99†11 ). SR-WM binds soft visual entities to five typed roles: gripper, target, goal, relation, and phase. The first three ground the embodiment and task entities; relation represents geometric and symbolic predicates; and phase tracks progress. SAM-family proposals may bias entity attention (cite105†4 ; cite106†13 ), but proposal dropout preserves mask-free inference.\nL123: The shared role state directly conditions a Flow-Matching (FM) action head and an action-conditioned dynamics model. For each candidate chunk, the latter predicts contact, grasp, predicate establishment and preservation, fixture state, and phase change. A stage-aware selector ranks these transitions; for a low-confidence candidate, it retains the valid prefix and resamples the suffix from the first violation. Shuffled, reversed, and cross-trajectory negatives enforce sensitivity to causal action order.\nL124: Our contributions are:\nL125: \nL126:   * •\nL127: \nL128: We introduce task-conditioned role binding that maps visual entities to five functional roles encoding semantics, geometry, and progress.\nL129: \nL130:   * •\nL131: \nL132: We use one role state for multi-candidate FM generation and semantic transition prediction, evaluating predicate establishment, preservation, and phase progress.\nL133: \nL134:   * •\nL135: We combine stage-aware selection with violation-aware suffix repair and factor evaluation into representation, generation, selection, repair, transfer, and efficiency beyond Oracle@$K$ coverage.\nL136: cite107†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Systematic overview of our SR-WM framework. (a) Role-state construction pipeline: Multimodal observations (RGB history, robot state, and language instruction), optionally augmented with visual priors such as SAM proposals, are encoded by LeWM into soft entity tokens, which are organized into a structured role state.\nL137: (b) Task-role binding: Exchangeable visual entities are bound to explicit task roles (e.g., Gripper, Target, and Goal) conditioned on the task and robot state, while relation and phase states capture structured task semantics. (c) Semantic rollout: The semantic world model predicts task-critical events and predicates—such as contact, grasp, inside, and phase progression—for candidate actions, rather than high-dimensional future pixels.\nL138: (d) Semantic planning: A semantic selector ranks candidate actions, executes the best prefix, and repairs violating suffixes through a receding-horizon execute-observe-replan loop.\nL139: ## 2 Related Work\nL140: #### World models for action planning.\nL141: Visual world models support test-time optimization through predicted pixels, features, or latents. DINO-WM predicts DINOv2 features for zero-shot goal planning (cite98†16 ); LeWM learns joint embeddings with a 5M ViT-Tiny encoder and 10M predictor and reports up to $48\\times$ faster planning than foundation-model world models (cite99†11 ). RC-aux adds multi-horizon reachability supervision (cite100†6 ).\nL142: These models align prediction with planning but do not explicitly represent predicates that an action must establish or preserve. SR-WM adds typed task roles and a role-conditioned action head to this lightweight foundation.\nL143: #### Object-centric manipulation.\nL144: Slot Attention learns exchangeable entities (cite101†10 ); FOCUS, VIOLA, and Slot-MPC use object structure for exploration, visuomotor control, and differentiable planning (cite102†2 ; cite103†17 ; cite104†14 ). Recent world models also expose objects: OA-WAM uses persistent SAM3-derived slots (cite108†9 ); WorldDP uses SAM2 as privileged training guidance (cite109†3 ); MaskWAM predicts masks (cite110†15 ); and MRO-GWM evaluates with ground-truth masks and poses (cite111†5 ). These states remain organized mainly by instance.\nL145: SR-WM instead binds entities to functional roles used for generation and ablates masks as optional evidence.\nL146: #### Action proposal and semantic progress.\nL147: \nL148: Diffusion policies model multimodal receding-horizon actions (cite112†1 ), while nearest-neighbor policies demonstrate effective imitation from strong visual features (cite113†12 ). Neither candidate diversity nor a value head ensures the required transition. Our FM head conditions on roles before sampling, and the selector learns from transition semantics and hard negatives. We report generation, selection, and Oracle@$K$ coverage separately.\nL149: ## 3 Problem Formulation\nL150: \nL151: At step $t$, the robot observes visual history $O_{\\leq t}=\\{I_{\\leq t}^{v}\\}_{v=1}^{V}$, proprioception $q_{\\leq t}$, and instruction $\\ell$. These inputs define the role state used for prediction, generation, and selection:\nL152: \nL153:  | $$Z_{t}=\\left\\{z^{\\mathrm{grip}}_{t},z^{\\mathrm{tgt}}_{t},z^{\\mathrm{goal}}_{t},z^{\\mathrm{rel}}_{t},z^{\\mathrm{phase}}_{t}\\right\\}.$$  |  | (1)\nL154: \nL155: The FM head draws $K$ horizon-$H$ action chunks:\nL156:  | $$A_{t}^{k}=G_{\\psi}(\\epsilon^{k};Z_{t},\\ell),\\qquad\\epsilon^{k}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I),\\quad k=1,\\ldots,K,$$  |  | (2)\nL157: \nL158: where $A_{t}^{k}=(a_{t}^{k},\\ldots,a_{t+H-1}^{k})$ and $\\mathcal{A}_{t}=\\{A_{t}^{k}\\}_{k=1}^{K}$.\nL159: \nL160: For each candidate, action-conditioned dynamics predict role states at $J$ sub-horizon boundaries:\nL161: \nL162:  | $$\\widehat{Z}_{t+1:t+H}^{\\,k}=F_{\\theta}\\!\\left(Z_{t},E_{a}(A_{t}^{k}),\\ell\\right),\\qquad k\\in\\{1,\\ldots,K\\},$$  |  | (3)\nL163: along with semantic transitions $\\widehat{Y}_{t}^{k}$. A stage-aware selector chooses:\nL164: \nL165:  | $$k^{\\star}=\\arg\\max_{k}S_{\\omega}\\left(Z_{t},A_{t}^{k},\\widehat{Z}_{t+1:t+H}^{\\,k},\\widehat{Y}_{t}^{k}\\right).$$  |  | (4)\nL166: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhat Changed and What Could Have Changed? State-Change Counterfactuals for Procedure-Aware Video Representation Learning (https://arxiv.org/html/2503.21055v2)\nciteturn24view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2503.21055v2\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 468\n"
